 ### 4.3 Install and release records (Q5; owner-ratified mechanism, 2 November 2026; EX_127 C05)
 
 MH-1 makes a catch-up forecast valid only if the method revision was installed before that draw's date; OFF-1 says this is checked against local install records, not Git history (E2R-R54: Git history is never relied on). Design, with the release event made its own record (EX_127 C05):
-- **From MHUP-A onward**, every code install writes one create-only **install record**, `src/methods/_common/contract_data/install_records/INSTALL_<PACKAGE>_V1.json`, by a small tool run after POST PASS and the suite, before staging (Appendix J step 12), so it is committed with the package: the package name, its manifest SHA-256, the UTC time and local date of the POST PASS, and the registry entries the package added. It records **code arrival**, which never by itself qualifies a variant for CATCH-UP.
+- **From MHUP-A onward**, every code install writes one create-only **install record**, `src/methods/_common/contract_data/install_records/INSTALL_<PACKAGE>_V1.json`, by a small tool run after POST PASS and the suite, before staging (Appendix J step 12), so it is committed with the package: the package name, its manifest SHA-256, the UTC time and local date at which it is written (step 12, after POST PASS and the suite; the field keeps its name `post_pass_utc`), and the registry entries the package added. It records **code arrival**, which never by itself qualifies a variant for CATCH-UP.
 - Every package that releases variants also writes, **after COMMITTED PASS and after E and P are removed** (Appendix J step 16; the updater lock is released only after the release commit), a create-only **release record**, `RELEASE_<PACKAGE>_V1.json`, by the same tool: the released (game, method, revision, variant) entries, the UTC time and local date of that step, and the install record's digest; the owner commits it as the package's second small commit before the push, and the verifier's `released` phase checks it. **The release date used by MH-1's rule is the release record's local date**, read from the PC clock when the record is written (clock-dependent, like the deadline, 6.4). A variant with an install record and no release record is not released and gets no CATCH-UP; an install that STOPs after POST and never releases therefore qualifies nothing.
-- **The baseline record**, `RELEASE_BASELINE_20261031_V1.json`, travels in MHUP-A as an ordinary reviewed member: 40 entries, the 39 variants released on 31 October 2026 (REL-1 at `6a1bed9` and WF-1A at `0d98706`; register v0.23 lines 1183 and 1189) and E_1 revision 2 (E1R2 at `86d12ea`, line 1215) marked `superseded_by: revision 3`; the dates are the days the variants became released, not the earlier dates their non-runnable code was installed. **The baseline is the one exception to "a release record's date is the day it is written":** it is written with MHUP-A in November and carries the owner-confirmed 31 October dates, validated against register v0.23 lines 1183, 1189 and 1215; the owner's installation of MHUP-A is his confirmation of the record, and no later record may carry a date before its own writing.
+- **The baseline record**, `RELEASE_BASELINE_20261031_V1.json`, travels in MHUP-A as an ordinary reviewed member: 40 entries per game (80 in all), for each game the 39 variants released on 31 October 2026 (REL-1 at `6a1bed9` and WF-1A at `0d98706`; register v0.23 lines 1183 and 1189) and E_1 revision 2 (E1R2 at `86d12ea`, line 1215) marked `superseded_by: revision 3`; the dates are the days the variants became released, not the earlier dates their non-runnable code was installed. **The baseline is the one exception to "a release record's date is the day it is written":** it is written with MHUP-A in November and carries the owner-confirmed 31 October dates, validated against register v0.23 lines 1183, 1189 and 1215; the owner's installation of MHUP-A is his confirmation of the record, and no later record may carry a date before its own writing.
 - **The rule:** a catch-up forecast for draw N by a variant is CATCH-UP if the variant's release date (local calendar date) is before N's derived draw date; otherwise it is development evidence. **Validation:** each record carries its own digest; every entry it names must have a registry chain entry, active or superseded, for exactly that (game, method, revision, variant); two release records naming the same entry refuse at validation; a variant with no release record is ineligible for CATCH-UP (its forecasts are development evidence), never an error; no record ever manufactures retroactive standing (a release record's date can only be the day it was written, the baseline excepted as above). **Retired:** a variant is retired when its registry chain entry is superseded (as E_1 revision 2 by revision 3) or marked retired by an installed package; the session roster is computed at `open-session` from the release records minus the retired entries, and a retirement takes effect at the next `open-session`, never inside a session. Release records and retirements arrive only by an install, and an install that changes the roster refuses while any weekly set is unfinished (Appendix J, the install check, steps 2 and 16; EX_129 C01), so no roster changes while a forecast set is unfinished; the owner finishes the set by running the weekly session (6.3). An install that changes no roster (an empty release record, a fix) is not refused on this ground, so a defect that stops a set from finishing can always be fixed by a package.
 
